# Typesetting the book

A print renderer turns a validated publication document into a physical layout.
This is more than changing HTML tags into LaTeX commands. Pages have finite
geometry, code must remain legible, links need stable destinations, and annotations
must find space without colliding with the body.

The architecture first builds a controlled document, then asks a trusted converter
for a LaTeX body, and finally surrounds that body with a renderer-owned book
template. Author text never becomes template control data.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Document[Validated publication document] --> Transform[Renderer-controlled transformations]
    Transform --> Convert[Trusted prose-to-LaTeX conversion]
    Convert --> Template[Book template and layout policy]
    Template --> TeX[Complete TeX document]
```

The arrows show increasing commitment to print. Structured prose remains data until
the conversion step; geometry and font commands enter only in the template. The
diagram omits compilation, which belongs to the next stage and must not influence
document meaning.

## Geometry begins with a readable body

Paper size and margins define the ordinary text rectangle. For page width `W`, left
margin `L`, right margin `R`, and an annotation reserve `A`, the usable body width is

```text
W_body = W - L - R - A
```

For an unannotated book, `A` is zero. When the annotation rail is present, the
reserve includes both rail width and the gap separating it from prose. On a page
210 mm wide, 20 mm left and right margins plus a 30 mm rail reserve leave a 140 mm
body: `210 - 20 - 20 - 30 = 140`. Height is similarly
`H_body = H - T - B`, using top and bottom margins.

The renderer validates margins and requires a minimum body in both dimensions.
This catches combinations that are individually plausible but collectively leave
no readable page. Geometry validation occurs before conversion because no prose
transformation can repair an impossible rectangle.

The rail reserve is added to the physical right margin rather than taken silently
from the promised body width. Body protrusion is disabled for annotated books so
optical margin behavior cannot enter the clear gap.

## Font roles express purpose

The template distinguishes body, heading, code, fragment-label, and note families.
A project setting resolves each role, and a caller may provide validated overrides.
The note role is resolved explicitly rather than accidentally following every later
body-font override.

Code ligatures are a policy choice. They may improve the appearance of some fonts,
but can also make literal character sequences harder to inspect. The renderer
therefore treats the choice as a checked boolean and can disable contextual
substitutions without accepting arbitrary font feature strings.

Portable font companions, when present, provide explicit regular, bold, italic, and
bold-italic faces. Without companions, family names select installed fonts. In both
modes, author text cannot inject paths or font options through a family name.

## Headings and references share semantic targets

Document, heading, fragment, contribution, term, note, and use identities already
exist before typesetting. The LaTeX renderer encodes them again into a private label
namespace. This second encoding ensures that even a manually constructed model
cannot place TeX syntax inside a command argument.

Visible fragment names are escaped independently from destinations. Long names gain
discretionary break opportunities between characters, allowing line wrapping
without changing the semantic name or destination. Names that exceed the renderer's
explicit bound fail instead of being silently abbreviated.

References use the family target, while individual contribution destinations remain
available for navigation and indexes. The visible fragment spelling stays intact;
the printed link and page reference are presentation around that spelling, not a
replacement for it.

## Code needs full context

Syntax highlighting works best when a highlighter sees a complete contribution.
Highlighting each literal segment separately would lose language context around
fragment references. The renderer therefore highlights the full display text and
then overlays semantic reference ranges, just as screen rendering does.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Code[Complete displayed contribution] --> Highlight[Syntax spans]
    Code --> Meaning[Reference spans]
    Highlight --> Overlay[Print span overlay]
    Meaning --> Overlay
    Overlay --> LaTeX[Escaped styled code and links]
```

The arrows indicate two coordinate systems over the same UTF-16 text. Neither pass
parses the other's output. The diagram omits ordinary inline code, which follows a
simpler escaping path.

The renderer owns all raw LaTeX used for code boxes, colors, fonts, and links.
Literal source text is escaped. Unsupported control characters are rejected. The
trusted document converter has syntax highlighting disabled so there is only one
highlighting policy.

## The converter is narrow and trusted

Pandoc receives the already validated document tree and returns a LaTeX body. It is
not allowed to choose the standalone template, run filters, load external includes,
or add a second syntax highlighter. Trusted raw nodes inserted by the renderer must
be preserved; raw author markup never reaches this boundary.

The implementation expresses this stage through `renderLatex`, which calls the
controlled document bridge and an injected converter. A converter failure, invalid
return value, or template failure becomes a diagnostic, and no partial LaTeX result
is returned.

This boundary is intentionally injectable. The pure renderer can be tested with a
controlled converter, while the command-line adapter separately owns the real
Pandoc process and its time and output bounds.

## Annotations require page-time knowledge

A term definition stays in the prose. A term use can link back to that definition
and display its page. A note use becomes a numbered marker whose body belongs in the
outer rail. Unlike ordinary links, rail placement depends on the physical page and
the vertical position produced by TeX.

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    participant Body as Typeset body
    participant Marks as Late page marks
    participant Rail as Annotation rail
    participant Ship as Page shipout

    Body->>Marks: Record term uses and note positions
    Marks->>Rail: Supply physical-page membership and coordinates
    Rail->>Rail: Place ordered entries without overlap
    Rail->>Ship: Release a stable page
```

The sequence emphasizes that logical source order alone cannot determine physical
placement. Late page nodes report where material actually landed. The diagram omits
auxiliary-file details; the important requirement is that the final build certifies
stable placement.

Within a page, the rail preserves one ordered stream of term uses and notes. It
tries to place each entry near its recorded position; forward and reverse packing
may move entries up or down to avoid overlap and keep the complete stream inside
the page. If the page cannot accommodate the required material, the build must fail
or rerun rather than quietly overlap or discard an annotation.

Annotation bodies are converted through the same trusted prose writer. They are not
flattened to plain text. Bounds on declaration count, individual controlled values,
and aggregate content keep repeated conversion and rail construction finite.

## Indexes summarize identities

Annotated books receive a term index and a fragment index. Term entries point to
prose definitions. Fragment entries list the pages of the family's accepted
contributions. Index generation consumes explicit model order and targets; it does
not scan the LaTeX body for labels.

Indexes are appended only after the main body transformation has succeeded. They do
not replay term-use markers or annotation bodies, which could otherwise distort the
rail or duplicate definitions.

## Images are page-aware blocks

A supported standalone image becomes a non-floating block. The renderer measures
its escaped caption at the current line width, subtracts the caption and spacing
from the full text-block height, and fits the graphic within that maximum height and
the current width while preserving aspect ratio. TeX moves the indivisible block to
a later page when the current page has insufficient remaining space.

This matters inside lists and quotations, where the current line width can be
narrower than the page body. A caption that leaves no useful height is an explicit
error. The block may move intact to the next page rather than splitting image from
caption.

The renderer receives only a safe companion path from the resource planner. It does
not resolve author filesystem paths or invoke conversion tools.

## A controlled template, not a custom execution surface

The final template owns the document class, packages, geometry, font declarations,
colors, heading behavior, code environment, image macro, links, and optional rail.
Only validated layout, palette, font, and companion-path values enter it.

This does not make TeX a sandbox. The generated document is trusted compiler input.
The safety property is narrower: arbitrary author Markdown is represented through a
controlled tree and escaped text rather than copied into raw TeX commands.

The result of this chapter is a complete in-memory TeX document and its known
companion requirements. The next chapter explains how images, diagrams, fonts, and
licenses become portable resources without giving author paths authority over the
publication layout.
